text: <|channel|>analysis<|message|>We need to explain that hot water can kill yeast, reducing fermentation. Also mention that too hot water can denature gluten proteins, affecting dough structure. Also mention that yeast is sensitive to temperature; optimum ~35-40°C (95-104°F). If water > 50°C (122°F) it can kill yeast. Also mention that if water is too hot, the dough may not rise properly, resulting in dense bread. Also mention that hot water can cause the dough to become sticky, overmixing, etc. Provide details. Also mention that if water is too cold, yeast activity slows. Provide explanation. Also mention that the water temperature can affect gluten development. Provide a concise answer.<|end|><|start|>assistant<|channel|>final<|message|>**Hot water can hurt the yeast and the dough**

| What happens | Why it matters |
|--------------|----------------|
| **Yeast death** | Most baker’s yeast is alive and active at 35–40 °C (95–104 °F). If the water you add is above ~50 °C (122 °F), the yeast cells are killed or severely stressed, so they can’t ferment the sugars. The dough will rise very little or not at all. |
| **Gluten denaturation** | Gluten proteins (gliadin and glutenin) start to unfold and lose elasticity when exposed to temperatures above ~60 °C (140 °F). Hot water can make the dough too soft or sticky, making it hard to develop a proper gluten network that traps the CO₂. |
| **Flavor loss** | Yeast also produces flavor‑contributing compounds (esters, aldehydes). If the yeast is killed early, those compounds are not produced, giving a bland loaf. |
| **Texture changes** | A dough that hasn’t risen properly will be dense, with a tight crumb. The crumb may also be gummy if the gluten didn’t set correctly. |

**Bottom line:**  
Use water that’s warm to the touch (about 38–45 °C / 100–113 °F). If you’re unsure, test the temperature with a kitchen thermometer or by feeling the water with your hand—if it feels hot, let it cool a bit before mixing. This keeps the yeast alive and gives the dough a good structure to rise.
